Original commit body from 66737979dee9a56daeebf55731181ddf057ac53c:

The suite's only full-history git walk took 1,185,559 ms in CI and 27 ms on a
dev box. The command was never the problem; the checkout was.

woodpeckerci/plugin-git defaults `partial: true`, and its own defaults.go then
sets Depth=1 and filter="tree:0", so the workspace was a promisor repository
holding no tree objects beyond the single commit it checked out. `git fetch
--unshallow` in prepare-git restores the commit history but INHERITS the
configured filter, so the trees stayed missing. Commit-only reads never noticed
(dco's rev-list, merge-base, the recency window); a pathspec walk needs a tree
from every commit, so ~26,000 of them came back from origin one HTTPS round
trip at a time.

That is why every local reproduction failed: a locally-made shallow clone has
no filter, so it has all its trees. It is also why the reverted commit-graph
warm-up made CI worse rather than better — `--changed-paths` diffs every commit,
so it demanded the identical trees on every pipeline.

All three workflows now pin `settings: {partial: false}`. The trade is one
~264 MB bulk clone against ~26,000 round trips, which is the shape the file's
comment already claimed to have.

Guarded twice, because there are two failure modes:
  * tests/test_ci_clone_is_complete.py asserts statically that every clone step
    declares partial:false (absent IS the bug — the plugin default is true),
    covering both of Woodpecker's accepted `clone:` spellings.
  * prepare-git reads remote.origin.partialclonefilter at run time and fails the
    pipeline, because no static check can see the declaration silently ceasing
    to take effect after a plugin bump or a server-side override.
Both default-deny (L54): they require the safe value to be stated rather than
enumerating the filters known to hurt. Mutation-tested — removing either half
turns the corresponding test red.

Also reverts the 1800 s subprocess cap to 120 s. Raising it was worse than the
30 s it replaced: it let the walk finish, which pushed the pytest step past the
agent's task deadline and got the pipeline CANCELLED, trading a fast legible red
for an unattributable infrastructure error.

The new gate is registered in KNOWN_UNREACHABLE with its consequence stated: its
subject is YAML, the top-level mapper is a governance file, so editing a
workflow does not select it per-PR. The runtime half covers that gap.

